# The runner's resource envelope. One file, so tuning is one edit.
#
# Warden's CI is light: python unit tests run in ~1s, the js suite is `node
# --test` (no bundler, no browser), and the integration job just connects to
# four throwaway DB containers. There's no web build here, so none of gravity's
# V8-heap pressure applies and the caps can be modest.
#
# This Mac already gives ~2 cores to Defender/DLP and OrbStack's VM before CI
# starts, so keep the footprint small and leave the box to its owner.

# One runner. Warden's whole pipeline finishes in a couple of minutes, so serial
# jobs are fine and a second runner isn't worth the extra load on this machine.
RUNNER_COUNT=1

# Hard caps on the container, per runner.
RUNNER_CPUS=2
# 2g is plenty without a web build. A --memory limit is a cap, not a
# reservation, so this doesn't reserve anything; it just stops a runaway job
# from dragging the host into swap.
RUNNER_MEMORY=2g
RUNNER_MEMORY_SWAP=2g
# Relative weight under contention. Half the default 1024, so interactive work
# wins the core rather than merely sharing it.
RUNNER_CPU_SHARES=512
RUNNER_PIDS=512
# Scheduling priority inside the container. Higher is politer.
RUNNER_NICE=10

# In-build parallelism. A container sees the host's cpuinfo, not its quota, so
# say the number out loud and let pytest/node schedule for the space they have.
RUNNER_BUILD_PARALLELISM=2

# Where the runner checks code out. Deliberately the SAME absolute path on the
# host and inside the container, so a service container that mounts $PWD (the
# host daemon resolves that on the host) sees the real checkout. Each runner
# appends its index so two never share a work dir.
RUNNER_WORK_DIR=/Users/osamayusuf/.warden-runner-work
